zfsbackup (1.0.0~rc4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fourth release candidate of 1.0.0.
  * Local destinations: a destination with url zfs:<dataset> backs datasets
    up to another dataset or pool on the same host with zfs send | zfs
    receive, no server or TLS; copies are laid out and locked down like
    server copies and pruned by their own retention.
  * Fix: on OpenZFS 2.1 a raw receive skipped the forced sharesmb=off, so
    a raw server copy could inherit the target's sharesmb; it is now set
    after the receive.
  * A failure at one destination no longer skips the dataset's other
    destinations in that cycle.
  * libzfseasy: StreamHandle.terminate()/poll()/pid, and
    zfs.send.*(peek=False).

 -- Radoslaw Kamil Ejsmont <radoslaw@ejsmont.net>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000

zfsbackup (1.0.0~rc3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Third release candidate of 1.0.0.
  * Server copies now keep the client's pool name in their path
    (<target>/<client_id>/<pool>/<dataset>), so datasets of the same name in
    two pools of one client no longer collide.
  * Fix a race between two concurrent `trust token create` runs on a host
    without a server TLS identity.

 -- Radoslaw Kamil Ejsmont <radoslaw@ejsmont.net>  Sun, 08 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000

zfsbackup (1.0.0~rc2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Second release candidate of 1.0.0, the first one packaged: for Debian 13
    and Ubuntu 26.04, with distribution Python dependencies (no bundled
    virtualenv).
  * Binary packages: python3-libzfseasy (the ZFS bindings) and zfsbackup
    (the daemon and zfsbackup-config).
  * The services are neither enabled nor started on installation, and
    /var/lib/zfsbackup (configuration database, TLS keys) is never removed.

 -- Radoslaw Kamil Ejsmont <radoslaw@ejsmont.net>  Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
